"intro": "Femi, who runs a kart repair business; Jade, his solicitor; Marshal Joy, the race marshal in the control tower. Femi fixed a whole fleet of karts for a customer, who never paid the £18,000 bill. Femi sued, and the customer has just filed a defence. Now the marshal takes over the race...",
"steps": [
{
"say": [
{
"who": "Marshal Joy",
"text": "(leaning out of the tower) Defence filed! Here's your notice of proposed allocation. Fill in your questionnaire, please."
},
{
"who": "Jade",
"text": "We'd be happy to try mediation first."
}
],
"q": "What does Jade file?",
"opts": [
"A directions questionnaire on form N181, saying whether they want a stay to try to settle (usually for a month).",
"Form N180, the small claims questionnaire.",
"Nothing: the court picks the track by itself.",
"The DQ whenever it suits her, because a late DQ has no consequences."
],
"a": 0,
"why": "N181 is for every track except small claims, and the DQ asks about settlement and ADR.",
"whys": [
"",
"N180 is for the small claims track; £18,000 is over £10,000.",
"Each party must file a DQ by the date in the notice.",
"The court can order it within 7 days, failing which the claim may be struck out."
]
},
{
"say": [
{
"who": "Marshal Joy",
"text": "£18,000 and a one-day trial. Off you go!"
}
],
"q": "The claim is allocated on 1 March. Which track, and what timetable can Femi expect?",
"opts": [
"The fast track: disclosure in about 4 weeks, witness statements in 10, experts' reports in 14, trial within about 30 weeks.",
"The small claims track, with an informal hearing.",
"The intermediate track, with up to 3 days for trial.",
"The multi-track, with costs budgets and a CMC."
],
"a": 0,
"why": "£10,000 to £25,000 and a trial of no more than one day.",
"whys": [
"",
"Small claims is up to £10,000.",
"£18,000 with a one-day trial fits the fast track. The intermediate track is for claims that do not fit it (normally £25,000 to £100,000).",
"The multi-track is for claims over £100,000 or complex ones."
]
},
{
"say": [
{
"who": "Jade",
"text": "Our main witness is away. Could we have one more week for witness statements?"
},
{
"who": "The other side's solicitor",
"text": "Fine by us. I'll confirm in writing."
}
],
"q": "Can they do this without the court?",
"opts": [
"Yes, by agreeing in writing, as long as it doesn't change the trial, CMC, pre-trial review or checklist dates.",
"No: only the court can change any date.",
"Yes, and they could move the trial date the same way.",
"Only by applying once the date has passed."
],
"a": 0,
"why": "Those court-fixed dates are padlocked: changing them needs an application.",
"whys": [
"",
"Most time limits can be varied by written agreement (CPR 2.11).",
"The trial date can only be changed by applying to the court (CPR 28.3).",
"They can agree in writing now. And a party that applies before time runs out is judged on the overriding objective, not as relief from sanctions."
]
},
{
"say": [
{
"who": "Jade",
"text": "(groaning) My diary mixed up the dates. We served the witness statements two days late."
}
],
"q": "What happens now?",
"opts": [
"An automatic sanction applies: the witnesses may not give oral evidence without permission. Jade must apply promptly, with evidence, for relief from sanctions.",
"Nothing: a late witness statement has no consequences.",
"Femi's costs are automatically limited to court fees.",
"Femi's whole claim is automatically struck out."
],
"a": 0,
"why": "A late witness statement brings an automatic sanction (CPR 32.10).",
"whys": [
"",
"The automatic sanction applies without any order.",
"That is the sanction for a late costs budget (CPR 3.14).",
"Strike out needs an order or an unless order; the automatic sanction here is about the witnesses' evidence."
]
},
{
"say": [
{
"who": "Britney",
"text": "Marshal Joy lights the three Denton lights. The other side's solicitor: \"We object! Late is late."
},
{
"who": "Britney",
"text": "How bad? Why? What else?"
}
],
"q": "Will Jade get relief?",
"opts": [
"Very likely. The breach is not serious or significant (the trial date is safe), so although the reason is poor, all the circumstances favour relief. The other side should agree.",
"No: a diary mix-up is not a good reason, so relief must be refused.",
"No: any breach means the party gets only court fees.",
"Yes, and the other side was right to object anyway, just to be safe."
],
"a": 0,
"why": "Opposing relief for a minor slip can lead to costs penalties.",
"whys": [
"",
"Stage 2 is not the end. At stage 1 the breach is neither serious nor significant.",
"Court fees only is the automatic sanction for a late costs budget (CPR 3.14), not for late witness statements.",
"Opponents should not take advantage of minor slips."
]
}
],
